q('Which combination constitutes Virchow’s triad?', 'Venous stasis, endothelial injury and hypercoagulability',[
 ('Thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen and prolonged PT','These are laboratory features of consumption, not the triad.'),('Hemolysis, renal injury and thrombocytopenia','These describe a thrombotic microangiopathy pattern.'),('Hypoxemia, hypocapnia and tachycardia','These can accompany PE but are not its causal triad.')],
 'Venous thrombosis results from interacting flow, vessel and blood factors', '**Immobility, tissue injury and a prothrombotic state often overlap.**',['Cancer and inherited thrombophilia affect blood coagulability.','Surgery can combine several components.'], '**Use the mechanism to identify preventable risk.**',['Mobilization alone is not sufficient prophylaxis for every high-risk patient.','Balance preventive anticoagulation against bleeding risk.'],[['Stasis','Immobility'],['Endothelial injury','Trauma or surgery'],['Hypercoagulability','Cancer or thrombophilia']],[VT])
q('A patient has unilateral leg swelling and a likely DVT Wells score. Which investigation is the usual first-line imaging test for proximal DVT?', 'Proximal leg vein compression ultrasonography',[
 ('A positive D-dimer alone as definitive confirmation','D-dimer is nonspecific and requires an appropriate diagnostic pathway.'),('CT pulmonary angiography as the primary leg-vein test','It evaluates suspected PE rather than directly establishing proximal leg DVT.'),('A normal chest radiograph to exclude DVT','It does not assess leg venous thrombosis.')],
 'Clinical probability determines the diagnostic sequence', '**Failure of a vein to compress supports thrombus.**',['Use a validated imaging pathway and adequate technique.','If imaging is delayed, assess interim anticoagulation according to risk and guidance.'], '**A negative test must be interpreted within the whole pathway.**',['Some proximal-ultrasound pathways require repeat imaging when D-dimer is positive.','Do not treat a positive D-dimer as an anatomical diagnosis.'],[['Likely DVT','Prompt ultrasound'],['D-dimer','Sensitive but nonspecific'],['Negative initial scan','Follow the probability-based repeat pathway']],[NG],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A first proximal DVT occurred immediately after major surgery. The provoking factor has resolved and bleeding risk is acceptable. Which duration is commonly appropriate before reassessment and stopping?', 'Three months of therapeutic anticoagulation',[
 ('Seven days because surgery provoked the clot','That is inadequate treatment for proximal DVT.'),('Lifelong therapy for every surgery-provoked first event','A resolved major transient factor often supports stopping after primary treatment.'),('Stop as soon as swelling disappears','Symptoms do not define adequate treatment duration.')],
 'Primary treatment duration and indefinite secondary prevention are different decisions', '**A resolved major transient provoking factor lowers later recurrence risk.**',['Treat the established event adequately.','Residual symptoms or clot appearance alone do not mandate lifelong anticoagulation.'], '**Reassess at the planned treatment review.**',['Consider persistent risks, recurrence history and bleeding.','Document the reason for stopping or continuing.'],[['Surgery-provoked','Often finite treatment'],['Primary treatment','Commonly three months'],['Review','Persistent risk and bleeding assessment']],[NG,VT],style='clinical_scenario')
q('After primary treatment of an unprovoked proximal DVT, a patient has low bleeding risk. What is generally appropriate?', 'Consider extended anticoagulation with periodic reassessment of benefit, bleeding and preferences',[
 ('Mandatory cessation at six weeks in all patients','This is inadequate primary treatment and ignores recurrence risk.'),('Continue only if ultrasound shows residual thrombus','Routine residual-thrombus testing is not the sole decision rule.'),('Replace anticoagulation with aspirin as an equally effective option','Aspirin provides less protection against recurrent VTE.')],
 'The recurrence risk persists after an unprovoked event', '**Extended treatment is often favored when bleeding risk is acceptable.**',['The decision is not an irrevocable lifelong prescription.','Dose and drug choice depend on the clinical situation.'], '**Review the balance periodically.**',['New renal disease, bleeding or interacting medicines can change the plan.','Patient preference and adherence affect the achievable benefit.'],[['Unprovoked event','Higher recurrence risk'],['Extended therapy','Often favored if safe'],['Follow-up','Reassess changing risks']],[VT])
q('A patient with an unresected luminal gastrointestinal cancer and acute DVT has a substantial mucosal bleeding concern. Which anticoagulant-selection principle is best?', 'Consider LMWH rather than assuming every DOAC is preferable, accounting for bleeding, absorption and interactions',[
 ('All DOACs are universally contraindicated in every cancer','Selected patients can appropriately receive a DOAC.'),('Warfarin is automatically superior for all chemotherapy recipients','Interactions and practical issues often favor other approaches.'),('Cancer-associated DVT never requires anticoagulation','Untreated thrombosis carries important risk.')],
 'Cancer-associated thrombosis needs site-specific risk assessment', '**DOACs and LMWH are both treatment options in selected cancer patients.**',['Unresected luminal lesions can increase bleeding concern.','Drug interactions and impaired gastrointestinal absorption can favor LMWH.'], '**Reassess as the cancer and treatment change.**',['Renal function and platelet count affect the plan.','Active cancer often supports treatment beyond the initial phase when safe.'],[['DOAC','Selected patients with suitable bleeding and interaction profile'],['LMWH','Useful when mucosal bleeding or absorption is problematic'],['Duration','Linked to ongoing cancer risk and safety']],[CA],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A pregnant woman has confirmed acute DVT, normal renal function and no immediate delivery or procedural issue. Which treatment is usually preferred?', 'Therapeutic low-molecular-weight heparin',[
 ('Warfarin throughout pregnancy as routine first choice','Fetal risks make it unsuitable for routine pregnancy-associated VTE treatment.'),('Rivaroxaban as the established preferred pregnancy therapy','DOACs are not the recommended routine treatment in pregnancy.'),('Aspirin alone','It does not adequately treat acute DVT.')],
 'Pregnancy changes anticoagulant selection', '**LMWH is preferred for most pregnancy-associated acute VTE.**',['Dose is therapeutic and weight-based according to the regimen.','Renal impairment, delivery planning and neuraxial procedures require adjustments.'], '**Coordinate an obstetric anticoagulation plan.**',['Plan interruption around delivery rather than stopping unpredictably.','Postpartum treatment duration and feeding compatibility also require review.'],[['Pregnancy acute VTE','LMWH usually preferred'],['Warfarin','Not routine antenatal VTE therapy'],['DOAC','Avoid routine use in pregnancy']],[PR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with thrombotic APS is persistently positive for lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies. Which long-term oral anticoagulant approach is preferred for venous thrombosis?', 'A vitamin K antagonist, commonly targeting INR 2–3 for a first venous event',[
 ('Rivaroxaban because triple positivity improves its efficacy','Triple-positive APS has excess recurrence concerns with rivaroxaban.'),('Aspirin alone as equivalent secondary prevention','It is inadequate for established thrombotic APS in this setting.'),('No therapy once the antibody titer falls once','A single fluctuation does not erase the thrombotic history.')],
 'High-risk APS is an important exception to routine DOAC preference', '**Triple positivity identifies a higher-risk antibody profile.**',['Venous and arterial histories influence the treatment plan.','Anticoagulation quality and adherence require assessment if events recur.'], '**Use a specialist plan rather than extrapolating ordinary VTE trials.**',['Rivaroxaban should not be used in triple-positive APS.','Pregnancy requires a different regimen.'],[['Venous thrombotic APS','VKA usually preferred'],['Triple positive','High-risk profile'],['INR','Often 2–3 for first venous thrombosis']],[AP])
q('Following a thrombotic event, antiphospholipid antibodies are positive once during an acute illness. What is needed to establish persistent laboratory positivity?', 'Repeat appropriate testing at least 12 weeks later, with anticoagulant interference considered',[
 ('A repeat sample the next morning','That does not establish the required persistence interval.'),('Any isolated low-titer result as proof of APS','Transient or low-level positivity needs careful interpretation.'),('A prolonged aPTT alone without antibody testing','This is not sufficient laboratory evidence of APS.')],
 'Persistence distinguishes clinically relevant profiles from transient positivity', '**APS assessment combines the clinical event and appropriate antibody evidence.**',['Lupus anticoagulant assays can be distorted by anticoagulants.','Anticardiolipin and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I testing assess other parts of the profile.'], '**Plan repeat testing without unsafe treatment interruption.**',['Coordinate assay selection and timing with the laboratory.','Classification criteria support research consistency and do not replace clinical judgment.'],[['Interval','At least 12 weeks'],['Assay issue','Anticoagulant interference'],['Interpretation','Clinical event plus meaningful persistent profile']],[AP])
q('Which principle best reflects ASH 2023 thrombophilia-testing guidance?', 'Test selectively when the result can change management, recognizing that recommendations differ by provoking context',[
 ('Order a complete panel after every surgery-provoked DVT','Routine testing is not recommended for that setting.'),('Never test after any VTE under any circumstance','Selected contexts have conditional recommendations favoring testing.'),('A positive inherited test automatically mandates lifelong anticoagulation for every person','Management depends on the event, defect, recurrence and bleeding risks.')],
 'A test is useful only if its result changes a meaningful decision', '**ASH distinguishes surgical, nonsurgical, hormonal and other VTE settings.**',['Some recommendations are conditional and based on very low-certainty evidence.','Family testing is selective, particularly for known high-risk defects.'], '**Identify the management consequence before ordering.**',['Avoid tests during conditions that invalidate their interpretation.','Counsel about the limits of a positive or negative result.'],[['Routine panel','Often unhelpful'],['Selected context','May change duration or prevention'],['Evidence','Several recommendations are conditional']],[TH])
q('A patient on warfarin has low protein C and protein S during acute thrombosis. What is the best interpretation?', 'The results are confounded and do not establish inherited deficiency',[
 ('Both inherited deficiencies are proven by these measurements','Warfarin lowers vitamin K-dependent proteins.'),('Warfarin reliably raises protein C and S to exclude deficiency','It has the opposite relevant effect.'),('Repeat testing tomorrow on the same therapy always resolves the issue','The same confounders remain.')],
 'Natural-anticoagulant assays require suitable timing and conditions', '**Warfarin lowers protein C and protein S.**',['Acute illness and other acquired states can affect results.','Anticoagulants can also cause method-dependent assay interference.'], '**Arrange valid testing only when it can alter care.**',['Do not interrupt necessary anticoagulation without a safe clinical plan.','Use specialist interpretation and confirmation before labeling a family.'],[['Warfarin','Can lower C and S'],['Acute setting','Additional confounding'],['Diagnosis','Appropriately timed confirmatory evaluation']],[BS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which inherited defect can reduce the anticoagulant effect of heparin because heparin depends on that protein?', 'Antithrombin deficiency',[
 ('Factor V Leiden','It causes activated protein C resistance, not a primary lack of heparin cofactor.'),('Protein S deficiency','It impairs the protein C pathway.'),('Prothrombin G20210A','It is associated with increased prothrombin rather than loss of heparin cofactor.')],
 'Heparin amplifies an endogenous anticoagulant', '**Antithrombin inhibits thrombin and factor Xa.**',['Heparin accelerates this inhibition.','Acquired low antithrombin can occur in critical illness and protein loss.'], '**Investigate apparent heparin resistance systematically.**',['Check dosing, delivery and the monitoring method.','Elevated factor VIII can produce aPTT discordance without true inadequate anticoagulation.'],[['Antithrombin','Heparin-dependent inhibition'],['Resistance','Several biological and analytical causes'],['Assessment','Drug delivery and suitable assay']],[BS])
q('What is the principal mechanism of factor V Leiden-associated thrombophilia?', 'Resistance of activated factor V to inactivation by activated protein C',[
 ('Failure of fibrin cross-linking','That is associated with factor XIII deficiency.'),('Reduced VWF synthesis','That produces a primary-hemostasis defect.'),('Autoantibody-mediated factor VIII inhibition','That causes acquired hemophilia A.')],
 'A procoagulant factor can remain active longer when its regulatory cleavage is impaired', '**Factor V Leiden alters susceptibility to activated protein C.**',['It increases venous thrombosis susceptibility.','Risk differs between heterozygous and homozygous states and with acquired exposures.'], '**Avoid equating genetic risk with inevitable thrombosis.**',['A finding alone does not mandate anticoagulation in every asymptomatic carrier.','Consider estrogen, pregnancy and family history in appropriate counseling.'],[['Defect','APC resistance'],['Clinical association','Venous thrombosis'],['Decision','Combined genetic and acquired risk']],[BS])
q('The prothrombin G20210A variant is associated with which effect?', 'Higher prothrombin levels and increased venous thrombotic susceptibility',[
 ('A consistent severe factor II deficiency with bleeding','The usual association is increased prothrombin and thrombosis.'),('Loss of platelet GPIb','That causes Bernard-Soulier syndrome.'),('Complete absence of factor VIII','That causes severe hemophilia A.')],
 'Not every coagulation-gene variant produces a bleeding disorder', '**The G20210A variant affects prothrombin regulation.**',['It is an inherited VTE risk factor.','Clinical risk depends on zygosity and coexisting exposures.'], '**Test only when the result has a management consequence.**',['A negative result does not exclude all thrombotic susceptibility.','Duration decisions remain anchored to the clinical event and bleeding balance.'],[['Protein','Prothrombin'],['Association','VTE susceptibility'],['Management','Context rather than genotype alone']],[BS,TH])
q('An acutely ill medical inpatient is at high VTE risk but currently has active major gastrointestinal bleeding. Which prevention principle is most appropriate?', 'Use suitable mechanical prophylaxis while pharmacological prophylaxis is unsafe, and reassess when bleeding risk improves',[
 ('Give full-dose anticoagulation solely for prophylaxis despite uncontrolled bleeding','Treatment-dose anticoagulation is not routine prophylaxis and bleeding changes the balance.'),('Withhold every preventive measure throughout admission without reassessment','Mechanical measures and later pharmacological prophylaxis may become suitable.'),('Aspirin is automatically equivalent to LMWH for every medical inpatient','This is not the standard medical-inpatient prevention strategy.')],
 'Thrombosis and bleeding risks change over the admission', '**High bleeding risk can temporarily preclude pharmacological prophylaxis.**',['Use an appropriate mechanical strategy when feasible.','Device selection and contraindications require assessment.'], '**Reassess rather than making a permanent admission-day decision.**',['Start suitable pharmacological prophylaxis when safe and indicated.','Mobilization and avoidance of unnecessary immobilization also help.'],[['High VTE risk','Prevention needed'],['Active major bleeding','Pharmacological prophylaxis may be unsafe'],['Follow-up','Reassess changing balance']],[MP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('How should a DOAC interruption interval before a neuraxial procedure be selected?', 'Use drug-, dose-, renal-function- and procedure-specific guidance rather than a single interval for all anticoagulants',[
 ('Stop every anticoagulant for exactly six hours','Drug persistence and neuraxial risk vary markedly.'),('Use the INR alone to determine absence of all DOAC effect','INR is not a reliable quantitative DOAC assay.'),('Never interrupt any anticoagulant before a neuraxial procedure','Residual anticoagulation can create a serious spinal bleeding risk.')],
 'Procedural safety depends on residual drug effect and the consequence of bleeding', '**Renal clearance is particularly important for dabigatran.**',['Dose, last ingestion and interacting medicines also matter.','Neuraxial procedures require dedicated procedural guidance.'], '**Coordinate with the procedural and anticoagulation teams.**',['Account for catheter removal and restart timing as well as insertion.','Do not substitute an arbitrary clock rule for current guidance.'],[['Drug','Mechanism and half-life'],['Patient','Renal function and interactions'],['Procedure','Insertion, catheter removal and restart']],[AC,PR])
q('A patient taking apixaban has INR 1.0. Which conclusion is valid?', 'A normal INR does not exclude clinically relevant apixaban activity',[
 ('The drug has definitely cleared','INR is insensitive and reagent-dependent for this purpose.'),('The dose must be doubled to reach INR 2–3','DOACs are not titrated to the warfarin INR target.'),('Apixaban does not influence coagulation in vivo','Its anticoagulant action is direct factor Xa inhibition.')],
 'Warfarin monitoring cannot be transferred to direct oral anticoagulants', '**PT/INR response to factor Xa inhibitors is variable.**',['Drug-calibrated anti-Xa testing can quantify exposure when available.','Time since dose and renal function remain important.'], '**Order a test that answers the actual clinical question.**',['Emergency bleeding decisions may need action before a drug level returns.','Document the agent and last dose for the laboratory and clinical team.'],[['INR','Warfarin-focused measure'],['Apixaban','Direct Xa inhibitor'],['Quantification','Suitable drug-calibrated assay']],[AC],style='clinical_scenario')

